From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Tim Douglas <me@timdoug.com>
Date: Fri, 11 Sep 2026 19:30:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 25/25] spawn instead of vfork on NuttX

NuttX creates a task at an entry point. There is one address space and no
MMU, so nothing is copied and a child never runs the parent's code -- which
is why only 7 of its 18 architectures implement vfork() at all, and why
SYSTEM_TOYBOX is unbuildable on the other 11 despite its Kconfig not saying
so. Adding vfork() to an architecture is some 500 lines of stack-copying
assembly whose comments in every existing port admit the result is only
safe because the child does nothing but exec or _exit.

Spawning is what NuttX offers instead, and it is what NSH itself uses for
its own pipes and redirections: the descriptors the child would have set up
for itself in the window after fork() travel with the spawn as parameters.
exec_builtin() takes exactly those, so xpopen_setup()'s child block becomes
one call, and the parent half after it is unchanged.

A Toybox applet is not a NuttX builtin of its own -- they all live behind
the single "toybox" one and are reached as "toybox <applet> <args>" -- so
the resolution step prepends that where needed. Anything else is looked up
in NuttX's own builtin table, so "find -exec nsh" still works.

The three commands that call XVFORK() directly rather than through
xpopen_setup() get the same treatment: time and xargs here, and setsid,
which is left to be turned off in Kconfig because session leadership is not
a thing NuttX has.

Not carried over: xpopen_setup()'s callback argument, whose only user is
timeout's setpgid(). The child is no longer this process, so there is
nowhere to run it; timeout still works, its child just stays in this
process group.
